Compaction with RLE is broken. A few tests fail.

This is an easy failing test case: /usr/local/home/nbarnes/o/dev/freelist-rle/_runtest/runtime/ocamlrun /home/nbarnes/local/o/dev/freelist-rle/_runtest/ocaml -noinit -no-version -noprompt  -nostdlib -I /home/nbarnes/local/o/dev/freelist-rle/_runtest/stdlib -I /home/nbarnes/local/o/dev/freelist-rle/_runtest/asmcomp -I /home/nbarnes/local/o/dev/freelist-rle/_runtest/bytecomp -I /home/nbarnes/local/o/dev/freelist-rle/_runtest/compilerlibs -I /home/nbarnes/local/o/dev/freelist-rle/_runtest/driver -I /home/nbarnes/local/o/dev/freelist-rle/_runtest/file_formats -I /home/nbarnes/local/o/dev/freelist-rle/_runtest/lambda -I /home/nbarnes/local/o/dev/freelist-rle/_runtest/middle_end -I /home/nbarnes/local/o/dev/freelist-rle/_runtest/parsing -I /home/nbarnes/local/o/dev/freelist-rle/_runtest/toplevel -I /home/nbarnes/local/o/dev/freelist-rle/_runtest/typing -I /home/nbarnes/local/o/dev/freelist-rle/_runtest/utils -I /home/nbarnes/local/o/dev/freelist-rle/_runtest/toplevel  ocamlcommon.cma

The unswept large list is being corrupted in phase one of compaction.

Both 'owner' and 'next' fields of the last item on the list, at
0xfcf880, are corrupted, from
   {owner = 0x476d70, next = 0x0}
to {owner = 0x53,     next = 0x600}

Can't get rr to work, possibly because I'm running on the wrong sort
of machine. So gdb and some manual bisection.

That block at 0xfcf880 is not corrupted by compact_phase_one_mark, but
is corrupted before compact_run_phase reaches the end of
EV_COMPACT_EVACUATE.

In fact it's corrupted while evacuating the sizeclass 1 pools.

Three pools are on the evac list. The corruption occurs while
evacuating the second of these pools, at 0x7ffff63d0000.

When evacuating that pool, to_pool starts at 0x7ffff4320000
(gdb) p *to_pool
$99 = {next = 0x7fffed708000, next_obj = 0xfcd0b8, owner = 0x476d70, sz = 1 '\001', evacuate = 0, chunk = 4, chunk_size = 256}

Note that next_obj is not pointing inside the pool.

When we start evacuating size class 0, that pool looks like this:

(gdb) p *(pool*)0x7ffff4320000
$101 = {next = 0x7fffed708000, next_obj = 0x7ffff4320040, owner = 0x476d70, sz = 1 '\001', evacuate = 0, chunk = 4, chunk_size = 256}

which looks fine, but here's the free list in it:

0x7ffff4320040:	0x00000000000e3ffb	0x00007ffff4323b90

0x7ffff4323b90:	0x0000000000111bfb	0x0000000000fcce68

Note that the "next" field of the second free run is bogus.

When we first enter compact_run_phase, that pool looks like this:

(gdb) p *(pool*)0x7ffff4320000
$103 = {next = 0x0, next_obj = 0x7ffff4320040, owner = 0x476d70, sz = 1 '\001', evacuate = 0, chunk = 4, chunk_size = 256}

0x7ffff4320040:	0x00000000000e3ffb	0x00007ffff4323ba0

0x7ffff4323ba0:	0x00000000001117fb	0x0000000000000000

Which seems legitimate to me. Note that that pool is not close to the
head of the to_pool list.

Hmm. There seems to be some non-determinism here. It's still that pool
in question though. Starting from scratch, breaking just before we
start evacuating the first pool, the free list in that problematic
pool looks like this:

$140 = {next = 0x7fffed708000, next_obj = 0x7ffff4320040, owner = 0x476d70, sz = 1 '\001', 
  evacuate = 0, chunk = 4, chunk_size = 256}

0x7ffff4320040:	0x00000000000e67fb	0x00007ffff4323c30

0x7ffff4323c30:	0x000000000010f3fb	0x0000000000fcce68

But at the start of compaction it's fine:

$141 = {next = 0x0, next_obj = 0x7ffff4320040, owner = 0x476d70, sz = 1 '\001', 
  evacuate = 0, chunk = 4, chunk_size = 256}

0x7ffff4320040:	0x00000000000e67fb	0x00007ffff4323c40

0x7ffff4323c40:	0x000000000010effb	0x0000000000000000

Ah, this latter free block runs to the end of the pool:

0x7ffff4323c40 + 16 * ((0x000000000010effb >> 10) + 1) -> 0x7ffff4328000

Oh, look, the phase_one_mark code doesn't do the right thing with free
blocks: last_free[1] needs to be set to NULL.
